<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">camp gear</ansi>

Camp gear is a set of durable items worth the weight: each improves camping
in one clear way, and each is carried like any other item, in your pack, a
companion's pack, or the cargo (a horse will happily haul the heavy ones).
Gear is not used up the way a draught is: most of it never wears out. What
you take on the road is the decision.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The gear</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Bedroll</ansi>            2.5 kg   8 gold    never wears out
  One for each member who will sleep on one: <ansi fg="yellow">+25% Fatigue</ansi> recovered from a
  camp rest. Bedrolls go to you first, then to companions by number.
  Members without one recover normally; a bedroll is a bonus, never a need.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Tents</ansi>  (see Tents below)    never wear out
  One tent covers the whole company. Every tent counts as shelter for the
  rest and blocks the cold while the company rests; each trades something
  for something. <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> shows the tent at your camp.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Fire steel and tinder</ansi>  0.2 kg   5 gold   never wears out
  Damp firewood lights at the first try and burns with full warmth.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Iron cookpot</ansi>       3 kg    12 gold   never wears out
  <ansi fg="command">camp cook</ansi> makes one extra portion of a dish that takes more than one
  ingredient (hunter's stew, thyme-roasted game); single-ingredient dishes
  are unchanged (<ansi fg="command">help cooking</ansi>).

  <ansi fg="yellow">Camp bells and trip lines</ansi>  1 kg   6 gold   10 rests, then spent
  Strung round a resting camp, they give raiders away: with no watch posted
  there is a flat 20% chance to spot them, and with a watch the chance rises
  by 10 points, never above 90% (<ansi fg="command">help campwatch</ansi>). A rest uses one up.
  They also keep thieves out of the camp entirely (see Thieves below).

  <ansi fg="yellow">Field surgeon's kit</ansi>  1.5 kg   25 gold   5 uses
  At the end of a finished camp rest, a healer with mana left tends the worst
  lasting wound of the most hurt member, before the rest's bandages and
  splints, and the kit wears one use. It does nothing without a healer
  who knows Tend Wounds and has the mana (<ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>).

Provisioners (the markets of Dunmar and the Old Kings Road) sell all of it,
a little dearer on the road. They never buy gear back, tents included
(<ansi fg="command">help market</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Tents</ansi>

Four tents, one pitched at a time. Which one is pitched is your choice;
the pitched tent is fixed when a rest begins.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Oiled canvas tent</ansi>   9 kg    40 gold
  The plain shelter. Halves the weather's penalty to a rest
  (<ansi fg="command">help resources</ansi>); it does not stack with a shelter room: the better
  one applies. Everything else is as with no tent.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Fur-lined tent</ansi>      12 kg   90 gold
  Shuts the weather out of a rest entirely: no penalty at all, not just
  half. Heavier, and dearer.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Camouflaged tent</ansi>    7 kg    70 gold
  Raiders and thieves come to the camp half as often (the chance is
  halved as the rest begins). The cost is a light, wary sleep: the Rested
  buff lasts half as long, 7 and a half minutes instead of 15. It does not
  stop a raid that does come, and a watch still helps.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Large pavilion tent</ansi> 20 kg   120 gold
  Room for everyone to stretch out: a finished rest grants every member
  who slept the <ansi fg="yellow">Well Rested</ansi> buff (30 minutes) in place of Rested
  (<ansi fg="command">help inn</ansi>). It is easy to find: raiders and thieves come half again
  as often. A member on a duty still misses the buff, and the rest's
  wounds and Mana follow a camp rest, not an inn stay. It needs a pack
  horse of its own: 20 kg is a real share of a company's load
  (<ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>).

  <ansi fg="command">camp tent</ansi>                 lists the tents you carry and the one pitched.
  <ansi fg="command">camp tent</ansi> [canvas|fur|camouflaged|large]   pitches one you carry, at your camp,
                            between rests. <ansi fg="command">camp tent clear</ansi> goes back to the default.
  With no choice made, the first you carry is pitched, plain canvas first.
  A tent you chose but no longer carry is replaced by one you do. The
  choice lasts until the camp is broken. The web client's Company > Camp
  tab shows the pitched tent and a button for each tent you carry.

  Whether raiders or thieves come is rolled as the rest begins, so a tent
  changes the odds for that rest only. Bells and trip lines still keep
  thieves out whatever the tent. On a road with no raiders or thieves, the
  camouflaged tent only costs you rest; the large one is then free Well
  Rested.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How gear is counted</ansi>

  - Gear counts if your company has it at camp: in your inventory, a present
    member's pack, or the cargo. A separated, dead or away member's gear
    doesn't count.
  - It is counted when the rest begins, and the effects are fixed then: a
    bedroll lost mid-rest changes nothing until the next rest. Between
    rests, <ansi fg="command">camp status</ansi> lists the gear at hand and what it will do;
    the rest report and <ansi fg="command">camp status</ansi> during a rest list the gear in use.
  - You get one tent (of whichever kind is pitched), one cookpot, one fire
    steel and one set of bells at most: a second does nothing. Bedrolls
    count one per member.
  - Thieves never take camp gear, tents included (see below); it is only
    ever made use of.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Thieves</ansi>

On some roads (the Old Kings Road for now), a camp rest has a 20% chance of
drawing thieves who slip in while the company sleeps. There is no fight:
you find out when you wake, and are told what is missing. Starting a rest
there without bells warns you, and so does the web Camp tab. They take
about a tenth of the loose goods in the cargo and your companions' packs
(at least one item, never more than four). Breaking camp or resting again
straight away does not shake them off.

  - Never taken: anything equipped, your own pack, your gold and the
    treasury, quest items, keys, and camp gear.
  - <ansi fg="yellow">Camp bells and trip lines</ansi> strung before the rest mean thieves never
    come at all.
  - A posted watch may hear them first, with the same chance it has to spot
    raiders (<ansi fg="command">help campwatch</ansi>); a caught thief takes nothing.
  - Whether they come is settled as the rest begins, so a restart never
    rolls it again or robs you twice. If you are offline when the rest ends,
    you are told on your return.
  - Stolen goods are gone; they are not tracked down.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Fuel and embers</ansi>

A rest burns the fire down to <ansi fg="yellow">embers</ansi> when it finishes. Embers keep the camp warm
until you break it, but resting again needs the fire fed with <ansi fg="command">camp fire</ansi>,
which spends another firewood bundle, or the deadfall in a firewood room
(<ansi fg="command">help gathering</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>). Cooking needs a lit fire, not embers.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help cooking</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help campwatch</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help gathering</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>.
